<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Warsaw</h1><p class="st-meta">1807 CE – 1815 CE · capital Warsaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Napoleon's Polish client state, which revived a Polish government and army after the partitions and introduced the Napoleonic Code.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1806 and 1807, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> created the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-tilsit">Treaty of Tilsit</a> out of the lands Prussia had taken in the Second and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-partition-of-poland">Third Partitions</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-augustus-i-of-saxony">Frederick Augustus I</a>, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, its duke, linking it to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-saxony">Kingdom of Saxony</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The constitution of 1807 abolished personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, declared equality before the law and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-code">Napoleonic Code</a>, though nobles kept their land. Real power lay with French officials and the needs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon's</a> wars. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jozef-poniatowski">Jozef Poniatowski</a> built a Polish army that defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1809, after which the duchy gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Krakow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>About 100,000 soldiers from the duchy joined the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1812, and Poniatowski died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> in 1813. Russia occupied the duchy, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> divided it in 1815 into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/congress-poland">Congress Poland</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/grand-duchy-of-posen">Grand Duchy of Posen</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-city-of-krakow">Free City of Krakow</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-warsaw.jpg" alt="Duchy of Warsaw" width="320" height="325"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duchy_of_Warsaw_(1812).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1807 CE – 1815 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Warsaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick Augustus I, Jozef Poniatowski</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Grand Duchy of Warsaw, Napoleonic Duchy of Warsaw</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1811&amp;focus=duchy-of-warsaw" aria-label="Duchy of Warsaw on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5153 374 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Warsaw lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5473 630L5312 664M5473 630L5054 728M5473 630L5473 630"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5312 664h0M5054 728h0M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5473 630h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5473 630h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Warsaw. <a href="/atlas?year=1811&amp;focus=duchy-of-warsaw">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
